    <span class="sr-spoke-header-eyebrow">Things Fall Apart  ·  Spoke 04 of 05</span>
    <h1 class="sr-spoke-header-title">Key Figures</h1>
    <p class="sr-spoke-header-deck">The people whose decisions shaped the historical moment the novel inhabits, and the writer who named it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="sr-spoke-layout">
    <div class="sr-spoke-prose">

      <p class="sr-lede">The figures on this page are not characters in <em>Things Fall Apart</em> — they are the people whose decisions shaped the historical world the novel inhabits, and the writer who made that world visible on the page.</p>

      <div class="sr-figure-card" id="chinua-achebe">
        <h2>Chinua Achebe <span class="sr-figure-dates">(1930–2013)</span></h2>
        <p class="sr-figure-role"><em>Author of</em> Things Fall Apart</p>

        <p>Chinua Achebe was born Albert Chinụalụmọgụ Achebe on November 16, 1930, in Ogidi, in the Onitsha area of colonial Nigeria. His father was a catechist for the Church Missionary Society, and Achebe grew up in a household that was Christian but surrounded by the traditional Igbo culture his father had left to become a Christian. He was educated in both worlds simultaneously — a formation that gave him, as he later wrote, the vantage point of someone who could see both from the inside.</p>

        <p>He attended Government College, Umuahia — one of Nigeria's elite colonial secondary schools — and then University College, Ibadan, where he studied English. It was at Ibadan, reading the British novels that constituted the English literature curriculum, that he discovered his raison d'être as a writer. Joyce Cary's <em>Mister Johnson</em> — a novel about a Nigerian character who slavishly admires his white employer and eventually agrees to be shot by him — crystallized for Achebe what was wrong with Western writing about Africa. The novel was presented as a sympathetic portrait of an African. Achebe found it an insult.</p>

        <p>More broadly, he recognized that the entire tradition of European literature about Africa — including Joseph Conrad's <em>Heart of Darkness</em>, which he would critique directly in his 1975 essay "An Image of Africa" — rendered Africa as backdrop for European moral and psychological dramas, without granting Africans the status of full human beings with interiority, history, and culture. Africa existed in these books as a darkness, a primitive otherness through which Europeans moved and were tested.</p>

        <p><em>Things Fall Apart</em> was his response: a novel in which an African civilization is the subject, in which an African man's tragedy is rendered with the seriousness and complexity of any tragedy in the Western literary tradition, and in which the colonial disruption of that civilization is witnessed from the inside rather than narrated from above.</p>

        <p>In "The Novelist as Teacher" (1965), Achebe wrote that his goal was "to help my society regain belief in itself and to put away the complexes of the years of denigration and self-abasement." He was writing for two audiences simultaneously: Western readers who needed to understand that African civilizations had "poetry and dignity," and African readers who needed to recover pride in a past that colonial education had taught them to regard as primitive.</p>

        <blockquote class="sr-pullquote">
          <p>"I would be quite satisfied if my novels did no more than teach my African readers that their past — with all its imperfections — was not one long night of savagery from which the first Europeans acting on God's behalf delivered them."</p>
          <cite>— Chinua Achebe, "The Novelist as Teacher" (1965)</cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p><em>Things Fall Apart</em> was published in 1958 by Heinemann in London, two years before Nigerian independence. It has been translated into more than fifty languages and has sold over twenty million copies. It is the most widely read African novel in history.</p>

        <p><em>Things Fall Apart</em> was the beginning of a literary project that Achebe pursued for the rest of his life. He followed it with <em>No Longer at Ease</em> (1960), tracing Okonkwo's grandson as he navigates the corruption of postcolonial Nigerian bureaucracy; <em>Arrow of God</em> (1964), a study of the collision between colonial administration and traditional religious authority; and <em>A Man of the People</em> (1966), a satirical novel about political corruption in the years after independence. Together these four novels constitute a panoramic account of Nigerian experience from the pre-colonial past through the postcolonial present.</p>

        <p>The event that most shaped the rest of Achebe's life came in 1967, when the southeastern region of Nigeria — the Igbo homeland — broke away from the federation to form the Republic of Biafra. Achebe was already the most celebrated African writer in the world. He chose to stay. He served the Biafran cause as a roving cultural ambassador, traveling to Europe and the Americas to raise awareness of what was happening — a famine deliberately engineered by the Nigerian government's blockade, in which hundreds of thousands of Igbo civilians, many of them children, died of starvation. When Biafra fell in 1970, he maintained, for over forty years, a considered silence about the war's full horror, finally breaking it in <em>There Was a Country: A Personal History of Biafra</em> (2012), published a year before his death.</p>

        <p>In 1990, a car accident on a Nigerian highway left him partially paralyzed and confined to a wheelchair for the rest of his life. He moved to the United States, teaching at Bard College and then, from 2009, at Brown University as professor of Africana Studies. In 2007 he received the Man Booker International Prize for a body of work that had, as the judges put it, contributed to an achievement in fiction on the world stage. In 2004 he declined Nigeria's second-highest national honor — Commander of the Federal Republic — in protest at the state of affairs in his country.</p>

        <p>Achebe died on March 21, 2013, in Boston, Massachusetts, at eighty-two.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sr-figure-card" id="frederick-lugard">
        <h2>Frederick Lugard <span class="sr-figure-dates">(1858–1945)</span></h2>
        <p class="sr-figure-role"><em>High Commissioner of Northern Nigeria; Governor-General of Nigeria</em></p>

        <p>Frederick Lugard was the British military officer and colonial administrator who built the governing framework of colonial Nigeria and whose doctrine of indirect rule shaped, and ultimately catastrophically failed, the administration of Igboland.</p>

        <p>He served in the British Army in India, Egypt, and East Africa before being appointed High Commissioner of Northern Nigeria in 1900. He was an effective military campaigner and an ambitious colonial theorist. His greatest contribution to British colonial policy — the doctrine of <strong>indirect rule</strong> — was explicitly designed to govern a vast territory with minimal British administrative presence, using existing local authority structures rather than deploying expensive British officials throughout the interior. In the hierarchical Islamic north, this worked reasonably well. In the acephalous Igbo south, it produced the warrant chief disaster.</p>

        <p>Lugard amalgamated the Protectorate of Northern Nigeria and the Protectorate of Southern Nigeria into a single Colony and Protectorate of Nigeria in 1914 — an administrative convenience whose consequences persist to the present day. The borders he consolidated enclosed peoples who had no historical relationship to each other and in some cases had been enemies, creating a "Nigeria" whose unity was a British administrative fiction. The tensions that fiction generated have defined Nigerian political history ever since.</p>

        <p>He served as Governor-General until 1919, then returned to Britain, where he continued to write and advocate for indirect rule as colonial policy. He is one of the most consequential figures of British colonial Africa and one of the most controversial.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sr-figure-card" id="joyce-cary">
        <h2>Joyce Cary <span class="sr-figure-dates">(1888–1957)</span></h2>
        <p class="sr-figure-role"><em>Author of</em> Mister Johnson</p>

        <p>Joyce Cary was a British novelist who served as a colonial administrator in Nigeria from 1913 to 1920. His 1939 novel <em>Mister Johnson</em> — set in colonial Nigeria and featuring a Nigerian protagonist who enthusiastically embraces British colonial culture — was widely praised in Britain as a sympathetic and humanizing portrait of an African character.</p>

        <p>Achebe identified <em>Mister Johnson</em> as the immediate provocation for writing <em>Things Fall Apart</em>. He had read it as a student and found it deeply condescending. In Cary's novel, Mister Johnson's devotion to his white employer and his ultimate willingness to be killed by him were intended to be touching to the reader; to Achebe, they represented the precise failure of imagination he wanted to correct: the inability to grant an African character a full interior life, genuine agency, and the right to be the subject rather than the object of his own story.</p>

        <p>Cary does not appear in <em>Things Fall Apart</em>. But the tradition he represents — British literature about Africa written by British people who had been to Africa but could not see it on its own terms — is exactly what Achebe was writing against.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sr-figure-card" id="cms">
        <h2>The Church Missionary Society (CMS)</h2>
        <p class="sr-figure-role"><em>Anglican missionary organization; primary Protestant presence in Igboland</em></p>

        <p>The Church Missionary Society was founded in London in 1799 by evangelical Anglicans. It became the dominant Protestant missionary organization in West Africa, establishing its first permanent base in Nigeria at Onitsha in 1857 and extending its reach through Igboland over the following decades.</p>

        <p>In <em>Things Fall Apart</em>, the missionaries who arrive in Umuofia are not specifically identified as CMS, but the pattern of their arrival — first Mr. Brown's patient approach, building a church and a school; then the stricter Mr. Smith's confrontational posture — broadly reflects the mixed character of missionary enterprise in Igboland. The CMS trained indigenous clergy, established schools, and produced the first written materials in Igbo languages. These contributions were real. So were the institutional disruptions they enabled.</p>

        <p>The school that Mr. Brown builds in the novel is not a neutral gift. It is the mechanism through which Igbo children will be equipped to serve in the colonial administration, the means through which the governance structures the egwugwu represent will be staffed by people trained to work around them. Mission schools and colonial administration were inseparable in practice, whatever the missionaries' stated intentions.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sr-figure-card" id="thomas-dennis">
        <h2>Archdeacon Thomas John Dennis <span class="sr-figure-dates">(1869–1917)</span></h2>
        <p class="sr-figure-role"><em>Church Missionary Society; creator of Union Igbo</em></p>

        <p>Thomas John Dennis was a British Anglican missionary who came to Nigeria in 1894 and spent the rest of his life there. He learned Igbo, devoted years to its study, published a grammar and a literacy primer, and undertook the immense labor of translating the entire Bible into a language he had adopted as his own. He died in 1917 when the ship carrying him home to England was torpedoed by a German submarine. A suitcase containing a draft of his Igbo-English dictionary washed up on a beach in Wales; a fisherman found it, dried the pages, and returned them.</p>

        <p>The Igbo language has many dialects, some so distinct that speakers can barely communicate across dialects. When Dennis set out to translate the Bible, he needed a single written form that could be used across all of them. Working with a committee of Igbo-speaking assistants from 1906, he assembled what he called "<strong>Union Igbo</strong>" — a standardized written dialect constructed by combining elements from multiple regional varieties. The Union Igbo Bible appeared in its entirety in 1913. Because the missionaries controlled the schools, Union Igbo became the standard written form taught throughout Igboland.</p>

        <blockquote class="sr-pullquote">
          <p>"There is a problem with the Igbo language. It suffers from a very serious inheritance which it received at the beginning of this century from the Anglican mission... The standard version cannot sing. There's nothing you can do with it to make it sing. It's heavy. It's wooden. It doesn't go anywhere."</p>
          <cite>— Chinua Achebe, <em>The Paris Review</em> interview, 1994</cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Achebe's decision to write in English was not simply a concession to global readership; it was also a response to the fact that the written form of his first language had been distorted into something with no inherent music or cultural grounding. The living Igbo dialects he grew up hearing were orally rich; the written Igbo available to him was a composite that belonged to no organic oral tradition.</p>

        <p>In an ironic reflection of the continuing influence of both the Archdeacon's work and the British colonial era in general, Dennis Memorial Grammar School in Onitsha, one of Nigeria's most distinguished secondary schools, is named in his honor.</p>
      </div>

